Subject: Re: OrchidPay integration kickoff & a couple of technical clarifications

Hi Sasha — thanks, clear list. Short answers up front, then a compact snippet and an attachable checklist: 

- SDK parity: yes — our official SDKs (Node, Python, Java) use the same auth surface for hosted and Dedicated. For Dedicated we'll provide a tenant-scoped API key; switching is a config flip (no code changes required) if you keep API host configurable.

- Endpoint migration pattern: we recommend sticky-session headers and idempotency keys for stateful chat session calls. Send X-Client-Session: <session-id> and Idempotency-Key for any messages that update state. During cutover, route a percentage of traffic to the new endpoint and mirror responses for 24h to validate.

- Retries/timeouts: baseline suggestion for short prompts — set client request timeout to 30s and per-token stopwatch on the server to guard long loops. Retries: 2 retry attempts with jittered exponential backoff (initial 150ms, multiplier 2, max 1200ms). For streaming endpoints prefer connection-level retries (reconnect/resume) rather than re-sending full requests.

- Rate limits & smoothing: best practice is client-side leaky-bucket + server-side rate fencing on per-route basis. Also enable prefix caching for repeated prompts (we can advise cache TTLs once we see payloads). For batch spikes, use adaptive batching (pack smaller requests into a single inference call) — the SDK helper in the attachment shows a batching helper.

Snippet (Node, minimal server relay):

// server/proxy.js - minimal Relay + backoff policy (pseudo)
const Redwood = require('redwood-sdk'); // placeholder name
const client = new Redwood.Client({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_KEY, host: process.env.REDWOOD_HOST });

async function callModel(req, res) {
  const idempotency = req.headers['idempotency-key'] || generateId();
  const session = req.headers['x-client-session'] || '';
  const payload = { model: 'redwood/gpt-rl-1', prompt: req.body.prompt, session };

  // simple retry with jittered backoff
  let attempt = 0;
  const maxRetries = 2;
  while (true) {
    try {
      const result = await client.generate(payload, { timeout: 30000, idempotencyKey: idempotency });
      return res.json(result);
    } catch (err) {
      if (isRetryable(err) && attempt < maxRetries) {
        attempt += 1;
        await sleep(jitter(150 * Math.pow(2, attempt)));
        continue;
      }
      return res.status(502).json({ error: err.message });
    }
  }
}

